From 8888bf08b3ad088651848f71489d384f876b456a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 19:01:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix the photo lightbox rendering off-page under the
 page-enter animation
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Reported on /it/work/still-life: opening a photo from a gallery grid in
the lightbox turned the screen pale/blank with the photo centred oddly
on the page instead of the viewport.

Root cause, confirmed live in the browser: `.page-enter` (the wrapper
`PageTransition` puts around every page's content) animates `transform`
with `animation: ... both`. `both` keeps applying the animation's `to`
keyframe forever after it finishes, and per spec an element with a
running *or filling* animation on `transform` becomes the containing
block for any `position: fixed` descendant, for as long as that holds —
regardless of the keyframe's value (`transform: none` included).
Toggling this element's `animation-fill-mode` live in devtools flipped
its computed `transform` from `matrix(1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0)` to `none`
outright, and the lightbox (`fixed inset-0` in PhotoGallery) immediately
started filling the actual viewport (0,0,1920,945) instead of the
~6300px document height it had been resolving `inset-0` against.

Every page's content sits inside this wrapper, so this wasn't just the
lightbox: any `position: fixed` element rendered in page content (as
opposed to persistent chrome like the header, which sits outside
`PageTransition`) was affected the same way, for the page's entire
lifetime, not just during the transition itself.

Switching to `backwards` keeps the pre-animation state applied before
the animation starts — so there's still no flash of unanimated content
on arrival — but lets go the moment it ends. Nothing else styles this
element, so the state it's left in afterwards (opacity 1, transform
none) is identical to what `both` was holding; the difference is only
that the animation, and the containing-block side effect that comes
with it, actually ends.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_015hHvGTWvAaz9WDpZm7oPPi
---
 src/styles/globals.css | 18 +++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/styles/globals.css b/src/styles/globals.css
index 1bddbfe..5c56e29 100644
--- a/src/styles/globals.css
+++ b/src/styles/globals.css
@@ -84,8 +84,24 @@
   }
 }
 
+/**
+ * `backwards`, not `both`: a running or filling animation on `transform`
+ * makes the element a containing block for its `position: fixed`
+ * descendants for as long as the animation is in effect — `both` never lets
+ * go of that, since it keeps applying the `to` keyframe forever after the
+ * animation ends. Confirmed live on /it/work/still-life: the photo
+ * lightbox's `fixed inset-0` was resolving against this wrapper's full
+ * (multi-thousand-pixel) document height instead of the viewport, so the
+ * backdrop centred the photo on the *page*, not the screen — miles off
+ * whatever the viewer had scrolled to, with only the oversized pale
+ * backdrop actually on screen. `backwards` only reaches back before the
+ * animation starts (so there's no flash of the un-animated state on
+ * arrival) and lets go the moment it finishes; nothing else styles this
+ * element, so what's left afterwards is the same opacity 1 / transform
+ * none the `to` keyframe asked for anyway.
+ */
 .page-enter {
-  animation: page-enter var(--duration-slow) var(--easing-reveal) both;
+  animation: page-enter var(--duration-slow) var(--easing-reveal) backwards;
 }
 
 /* Cross-document navigations get the same dissolve for free. */
